EXT. SALT-LICK STAGE STATION - NEVADA TERRITORY - DAY

A dusty way station with a sagging porch, a hand-pump well, and a corral. A white salt flat shimmers beyond. A stagecoach rests with its team unhitched, horses nosing at a lick of gray rock.

On the porch sits OLD CASPER VANDERHOOF, 70s, in a rocker, whittling. Beside him, his granddaughter LUPE, 10, dangles bare feet off the boards. Across the yard the stagecoach driver, TOMÁS ESCOBEDO, 40s, leans against a wheel, hat tipped over his eyes. A passenger, the preacher JEDEDIAH HOLLOWAY, 50s, stands at the pump filling a canteen. Inside the open stagecoach door, the passenger MRS. PRUDENCE LATIMER, 30s, fans herself with a handkerchief. At the corral rail, a ranch hand named WYATT BROOKS, 20s, scratches a mule's ear.

Nobody hurries. A hawk circles overhead. Casper's knife shaves a long curl off the stick. Lupe watches it fall.

LUPE
Grandpa, what's it going to be?

CASPER
A duck.

LUPE
It looks like a spoon.

CASPER
That's a duck in a hurry.

Tomás pushes up his hat and glances at the sun. Jedediah finishes with the canteen and pumps the handle twice more just to hear it creak. Prudence steps down from the coach, shakes the dust from her skirt, and crosses to the porch, handing the handkerchief to Lupe, who wipes her nose and hands it back with great seriousness. Wyatt gives the mule a last pat and ambles over to join them, thumbs in his belt.

WYATT
Will she be rolling by noon?

TOMÁS
When the horses finish licking.

WYATT
Shame to rush them.

JEDEDIAH
The Lord rested on the seventh day.

TOMÁS
The Lord didn't have a schedule from the railroad.

PRUDENCE
(smiling)
Then let us rest, gentlemen. I have nowhere I would rather not be.

CASPER
Spoken like a woman who's seen Reno.

PRUDENCE
Twice.

The hawk drifts lower. Lupe points. Everyone looks up. For a long moment the whole station watches the bird wheel against the bright sky, and then it banks and is gone over the flat.

CASPER
That's a duck, too.

LUPE
It's not.

CASPER
It's a duck in a hurry.
